  "fashion": {
    "Overview": "Corporate Grunge retains much of the fashion of original grunge, with minor tweaks. Most of the fashion of grunge is somewhat more polished and more accessible to a wider customer base. Oftentimes, grunge bands would dress in a wider range of different styles that are a mix of late 80s metal head and rural working class clothing, specifically from Seattle. A common fashion staple of grunge is the use of plaid shirts and distressed jeans. The plaid shirts come from Seattle's \"lumberjack\" look, which wasn't a fashion choice but a reflection of the regional working-class lifestyle. Oversized flannel shirts and thermal layers were chosen for warmth and durability during long winters. Grunge…"
  },
